From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 18:35:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 110/122] feat(refinery): restart every N patrol iterations per
 conversation (gcp-l8td.2)

The refinery's only restart trigger was "context feels heavy". Under
wake_mode = "fresh" that never mattered, because the controller kills the
session after every wisp burn. But that kill races the next bead and costs
~2.3 min per iteration (gcp-l8td design section A). The fix for the race is
wake_mode = "resume" (A1), and on 2026-09-24 A1 trialled alone let one
conversation carry every bead to 126k of its 200k window. This makes the
restart cadence a number so A1 can be re-enabled safely.

The count lives on the wisp chain, keyed by GC_CONTINUATION_EPOCH, which gc
bumps exactly when a conversation is reset and keeps across a resume wake:

- New var restart_after_iterations (default 2: ~64k start + ~55k per bead
  fits 200k; 1 = every bead; 0 = off; non-integer = WARN, then off).
- A session-iteration-stamp block at all four pours that continue the
  conversation: rebase reject, handle-failures reject, merge-push
  block_existing_pr, next-iteration section 2. It reads CURRENT's
  session_epoch/session_iterations and stamps NEXT with k+1 (same epoch) or
  1 (other or no epoch), in the same update that assigns NEXT. With the
  epoch unset it writes neither key. The reject paths stamp too, since
  they are the heaviest iterations and resetting the count on them would
  let a conversation overrun the window. check-inbox's own restart pour
  does NOT stamp: it starts a new conversation, and the epoch bump makes
  its unstamped wisp read as one.
- A restart-cadence-check block opening check-inbox section 1 prints one
  verdict line (RESTART DUE, k/N, or off (why)). The existing restart
  block now runs on RESTART DUE, a gc context advisory, or heavy context.
  The block itself is unchanged.

The sentinels around the stamp enclose the assign-if/fi as well as the
read and compute. The stamp has to ride that update, and the test
executes that exact write rather than a transcription of it. The four
copies are identical once leading whitespace is stripped. The command
text uses the formula's existing `set -- "$@"` idiom for conditional
args (as run_approval_gate does), not a bash array.

prompt.template.md section 2 now says the cadence is objective and points
at check-inbox and the var, without restating commands.

Under wake_mode = "fresh" (every rig today) this is inert at the default:
each burn starts a new epoch, so k never reaches 2.

Validation: gastown/tests/test_refinery_restart_cadence.sh extracts all
five blocks from the formula, asserts their placement and identity, and
executes the cadence check (acceptance cases a-f plus unstamped and
unreadable wisps) and the stamp (continue, new epoch, unstamped, no
epoch, unreadable CURRENT, failed assign) against a mutating gc stub.

